.Dd September 23, 2026
.Dt ICOWM-MSG 1
.Os IcoWM 1.0.1
.Sh NAME
.Nm icowm-msg
.Nd command-line client for the IcoWM control socket
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar command
.Op Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value ...
.Nm
.Fl w Ar events
.Op Fl n Ar count
.Nm
.Op Fl hvKW
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a small, standalone client for
.Xr icowm 1 Ns 's
control socket.
It builds one JSON request line out of its arguments, sends it to a
running
.Xr icowm 1 ,
and prints back whatever answer comes.
.Pp
.Ar command
is any command name the server knows, listed under
.Sx COMMANDS
below and obtainable from the running build with
.Fl K ;
.Fl K
lists them.
Each
.Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
becomes one field of the request object alongside
.Ql cmd ,
naming that command's argument.
.Pp
The response is printed to standard output exactly as received, on one
line, whether the command succeeded or not.
.Pp
.Nm
is built and installed alongside
.Xr icowm 1
as an entirely separate binary: building either never forces a rebuild
of the other.
It is independent at the source level too, linking only against
.Sy cjson
and never touching X11, XCB or any font library, since an IPC client has
no reason to pull in what a window manager needs only in order to be
one.
.Ss Options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl h
Print usage, a few examples and this option list, then exit.
.It Fl K
List every command name this build knows about, one per line, then exit.
.It Fl n Ar count
Stop watching after
.Ar count
events.
Only meaningful together with
.Fl w ;
on its own it is rejected, having nothing to count.
.It Fl v
Print this program's name,
.Xr icowm 1 Ns 's
short name and version, its license, its copyright line and its author,
then exit.
.It Fl W
List every event name this build knows about, one per line, then exit.
These are the same names
.Fl w
accepts.
.It Fl w Ar events
Subscribe to
.Ar events
instead of sending a command; the names it takes are listed under
.Sx EVENTS
below.
See
.Sx Watching for events
below.
.El
.Pp
The lists
.Fl K
and
.Fl W
print are hand-maintained snapshots of the server's own tables, kept
here so that both work offline, the same way
.Fl h
and
.Fl v
already do, rather than needing a running server to query.
They can therefore fall behind the server over time.
Neither is ever used to validate a command or an event name before
sending it: what is typed reaches the server unfiltered, so a stale
listing makes only its own output incomplete, and never rejects
something the server would have accepted.
.Ss Argument values
Every
.Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
is split on its first
.Ql = ,
and the value half is sent as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm true No or Cm false
A JSON boolean, when that is the whole value.
.It A number
A JSON number, when the value parses in full as one, for instance
.Ql 25165831 ,
.Ql -5
or
.Ql 3.5 .
.It A hexadecimal number
A JSON number holding its decimal value, when the value parses in full
as one prefixed
.Ql 0x ,
for instance
.Ql 0x1800007
or
.Ql 0xbadbabe .
.It Anything else
A JSON string, verbatim.
.El
.Pp
A numeric identifier therefore never needs quoting, and the
.Ql 0x
form is a convenience for values more naturally read that way, a window
identifier or a packed
.Ql RRGGBB
color: it reaches the server exactly as its decimal equivalent would,
JSON having no hexadecimal syntax of its own to carry it in.
.Pp
Note the other side of this: a value that looks like a number but is
meant as text, a client's new name made only of digits say, is sent as
a number.
No command currently has an argument this could affect.
.Pp
An argument not in
.Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
form at all, or with an empty key, is rejected before anything is sent,
naming the offending argument on standard error.
.Ss Watching for events
.Nm Fl w Ar events
leaves the one-request, one-response flow entirely: rather than sending
a command, it subscribes and prints one JSON line per event as they
arrive, for as long as the connection stays open.
A
.Ar command
and any
.Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
arguments are unused in this mode.
.Pp
.Ar events
is a comma-separated list of event names, without spaces, such as
.Ql window_mapped,desktop_switched .
With
.Fl n Ar count ,
watching stops once that many events have arrived; without it, watching
continues until the connection drops or the process is killed.
.Sh COMMANDS
Every command the server knows, in the groups the dispatch table
itself keeps them in.
An argument in brackets may be left out, and every one is given as
.Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value ,
in any order.
For what each response carries back, see
.Pa icowm-msg.md ,
installed alongside this page.
.Ss Queries
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic get_version
Reports the wire protocol version
.It Ic list_desktops
Lists every desktop on every managed surface
.It Ic list_clients
Lists every managed client, with geometry and state flags
.It Ic get_focused
Reports the active client of every managed surface
.El
.Ss Closing and focusing a client
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic close_client Ar client_id
Closes the client politely, or destroys its window
.It Ic kill_client Ar client_id
Forcibly terminates the client's X connection
.It Ic deiconify_client Ar client_id
Restores the client if it was iconified
.It Ic focus_client Ar client_id
Moves input focus to the client (does not raise it)
.It Ic unfocus_client Ar client_id
Takes input focus away from the client
.El
.Ss Iconifying and hiding a client
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic iconify_client Ar client_id
Iconifies (minimizes) the client
.It Ic hide_client Ar client_id
Hides the client without iconifying it
.It Ic unhide_client Ar client_id
Undoes `hide_client`
.El
.Ss Client flags
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic pin_client Ar client_id
Makes the client visible on every desktop
.It Ic unpin_client Ar client_id
Undoes `pin_client`
.It Ic toggle_pin_client Ar client_id
Toggles `pin_client`/`unpin_client`
.It Ic urge_client Ar client_id
Marks the client urgent
.It Ic unurge_client Ar client_id
Undoes `urge_client`
.El
.Ss Client geometry
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic move_client Ar client_id Ar x Ar y
Moves the client so its top-left corner is at that position
.It Ic center_client Ar client_id
Centers the client on its screen
.It Ic move_client_to_monitor Ar client_id Ar monitor_index
Moves the client to that physical monitor by its index, rather than by
compass direction
.It Ic move_client_to_monitor_north Ar client_id
Moves the client to the monitor north of its current one, resolved by
real physical position; a no-op with one monitor or none, or when none
lies to the north
.It Ic move_client_to_monitor_south Ar client_id
The same, toward the monitor south of the current one
.It Ic move_client_to_monitor_east Ar client_id
The same, toward the monitor east of the current one
.It Ic move_client_to_monitor_west Ar client_id
The same, toward the monitor west of the current one
.It Ic move_resize_client Ar client_id Ar x Ar y Ar w Ar h
Moves and resizes the client in one step
.It Ic resize_client Ar client_id Ar w Ar h
Resizes the client only, leaving position alone
.It Ic maximize_client_horz Ar client_id
Maximizes the client horizontally only
.It Ic maximize_client_vert Ar client_id
Maximizes the client vertically only
.It Ic maximize_client Ar client_id
Maximizes the client both horizontally and vertically
.El
.Ss Client layering
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic raise_client Ar client_id
Raises the client within its layer
.It Ic lower_client Ar client_id
Lowers the client within its layer
.It Ic set_layer_above_client Ar client_id
Moves the client to the "always on top" layer
.It Ic set_layer_normal_client Ar client_id
Moves the client back to the ordinary layer
.It Ic set_layer_below_client Ar client_id
Moves the client to the "always below" layer
.It Ic cycle_layer_client Ar client_id
Cycles the client through above, normal, and below
.El
.Ss Client metadata
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic rename_client Ar client_id Ar name
Overrides the client's window title
.It Ic reclass_client Ar client_id Ar class_name Ar instance_name
Overrides the client's `WM_CLASS`
.It Ic rerole_client Ar client_id Ar role
Overrides the client's window role
.It Ic set_client_icon Ar client_id Ar icon_name
Overrides which icon IcoWM shows for the client
.El
.Ss Client state
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic shade_client Ar client_id
Rolls the client up into just its titlebar
.It Ic unshade_client Ar client_id
Undoes `shade_client`
.It Ic toggle_shade_client Ar client_id
Toggles `shade_client`/`unshade_client`
.It Ic fullscreen_client Ar client_id
Makes the client fill its screen
.It Ic unfullscreen_client Ar client_id
Undoes `fullscreen_client`
.It Ic toggle_fullscreen_client Ar client_id
Toggles `fullscreen_client`/`unfullscreen_client`
.It Ic toggle_decorate_client Ar client_id
Shows or hides the client's titlebar and border
.El
.Ss Desktops and surfaces
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic set_desktop_background Ar desktop_id Oo Ar surface_id Oc Ar color
Sets that desktop's solid background color
.It Ic show_desktop Ar desktop_id Oo Ar surface_id Oc Ar show
Shows or hides every client on that desktop at once
.It Ic send_client_to_desktop Ar client_id Ar target_desktop_id
Moves the client to another desktop on the same surface
.It Ic send_client_to_front Ar client_id
Raises the client to the front of its desktop's window stack
.It Ic send_client_to_back Ar client_id
Sends the client to the back of its desktop's window stack
.It Ic iconify_all Oo Ar desktop_id Oc Oo Ar surface_id Oc
Iconifies every client on that desktop at once
.It Ic deiconify_all Oo Ar desktop_id Oc Oo Ar surface_id Oc
Restores every iconified client on that desktop at once
.It Ic rearrange_desktop Oo Ar desktop_id Oc Oo Ar surface_id Oc
Re-applies the configured placement policy on that desktop
.It Ic goto_desktop Ar desktop_id Oo Ar surface_id Oc
Switches the resolved surface to that desktop
.It Ic goto_north_desktop Oo Ar surface_id Oc
Switches the resolved surface to the desktop north of its current one
.It Ic goto_south_desktop Oo Ar surface_id Oc
The same, toward the desktop south of the current one
.It Ic goto_east_desktop Oo Ar surface_id Oc
The same, toward the desktop east of the current one
.It Ic goto_west_desktop Oo Ar surface_id Oc
The same, toward the desktop west of the current one
.It Ic add_desktop Oo Ar surface_id Oc
Adds a new desktop after the resolved surface's last one, growing its
configured grid layout by a row or column first if it does not already
have a gap cell for it
.It Ic remove_desktop Oo Ar surface_id Oc
Removes the resolved surface's last desktop, moving any client still
on it to the one before it, switching the current view there too if it
was the one removed
.It Ic toggle_strutless_maximize Oo Ar surface_id Oc
Toggles whether panel and tray struts are set aside when computing
that surface's work areas
.El
.Ss The window manager itself
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic toggle_scratchpad Oo Ar desktop_id Oc Oo Ar surface_id Oc
Launches the scratchpad, or shows/hides it if already running
.It Ic reload_config
Reloads every configuration file
.It Ic exit_wm
Requests that IcoWM stop and exit
.El
.Sh EVENTS
Every event that
.Fl w
accepts and
.Fl W
lists.
Each arrives as one JSON line naming the event in its
.Ic event
field, alongside the fields given here.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic window_mapped Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , Ar surface_id
A client was just mapped onto that desktop
.It Ic window_closed Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , Ar surface_id
A client was just destroyed
.It Ic desktop_switched Ar surface_id , Ar desktop_id
That surface's current desktop just changed to
.Ic desktop_id
.It Ic focus_changed Ar surface_id , Ar client_id
That client just became the active one on its surface
.It Ic urgency_set Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , Ar surface_id
That client's urgency hint was just set
.It Ic urgency_cleared Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , , Ar surface_id
That client's urgency hint was just cleared
.It Ic window_moved Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , Ar surface_id
That client's position just changed (see the
.Ic list_clients
command for its current
.Ic x
/
.Ic y
)
.It Ic window_resized Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , Ar surface_id
That client's size just changed (see the
.Ic list_clients
command for its current
.Ic w
/
.Ic h
)
.It Ic rule_applied Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , Ar surface_id
A loaded rule just changed one or more of that client's properties
.It Ic pin_set Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , Ar surface_id
That client was just pinned (visible on every desktop)
.It Ic pin_cleared Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , Ar surface_id
That client was just unpinned
.It Ic fullscreen_set Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , Ar surface_id
That client just entered full screen
.It Ic fullscreen_cleared Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , , Ar surface_id
That client just left full screen
.It Ic shade_set Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , Ar surface_id
That client was just shaded (rolled up into its titlebar)
.It Ic shade_cleared Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , Ar surface_id
That client was just unshaded
.It Ic hide_set Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , Ar surface_id
That client was just hidden
.It Ic hide_cleared Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , Ar surface_id
That client was just unhidden
.It Ic decoration_set Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , Ar surface_id
That client's titlebar and border were just shown
.It Ic decoration_cleared Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , , Ar surface_id
That client's titlebar and border were just hidden
.It Ic client_iconified Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , , Ar surface_id
That client was just iconified
.It Ic client_deiconified Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , , Ar surface_id
That client was just restored from being iconified
.It Ic layer_changed Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , Ar surface_id
That client's stacking layer just changed (see
.Ic list_clients
for its current layer)
.It Ic client_desktop_changed Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , Ar surface_id
That client just moved to a different desktop (here,
.Ic desktop_id
is the new one)
.It Ic client_renamed Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , Ar surface_id , Ar name
That client's displayed title was just overridden
.It Ic client_reclassed Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , Ar surface_id , Ar class_name , Ar instance_name
That client's
.Ic WM_CLASS
was just overridden
.It Ic client_reroled Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , Ar surface_id , Ar role
That client's window role was just overridden
.It Ic client_icon_changed Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , Ar surface_id , Ar icon_name
That client's displayed icon was just overridden
.It Ic desktop_background_changed Ar desktop_id , Ar surface_id
That desktop's solid background color was just set
.It Ic desktop_shown Ar desktop_id , Ar surface_id
Every client on that desktop was just shown at once
.It Ic desktop_hidden Ar desktop_id , Ar surface_id
Every client on that desktop was just hidden at once
.It Ic stacking_changed Ar client_id , Ar desktop_id , , Ar surface_id
That client's position within its layer's stacking order just changed
.It Ic config_reloaded
Every configuration file was just reloaded; carries no fields.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
.It Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Base directory holding the control socket.
.Nm
resolves the path the same way the server does, so the two must agree on
this variable to find the same socket.
When unset, both fall back to
.Pa /tmp/icowm-$(id -u)\& .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR}/icowm/socket
The control socket, created by
.Xr icowm 1
with mode 0700 on its containing directory, so nothing else on the
system can see it, let alone connect.
On a typical
.Xr systemd 1 Ns -managed
Linux system this resolves to
.Pa /run/user/$(id -u)/icowm/socket ,
where
.Ql user
is that literal word rather than any account name.
.It Pa /tmp/icowm-$(id -u)/socket
Where the socket lives instead when
.Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
is unset.
Note that this path has no
.Pa icowm/
component of its own: the directory is already named after the program
and the user, so only the socket name is appended to it.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
The command reached the server and it reported success, printing
.Ql \&"ok\&": true .
While watching, this instead means that
.Fl n Ar count
was given and that many events were printed.
.It 1
The command reached the server and it reported failure, printing
.Ql \&"ok\&": false ;
the reason is in the response's
.Ql error
field.
While watching, this means the subscription itself was rejected.
.It 2
The request never reached the server at all: no socket at the resolved
path, a connection failure, a response that does not parse as JSON, or
a local error such as a missing command, a malformed argument or an
unrecognized option.
Nothing is printed to standard output in this case; the reason is on
standard error.
While watching, this also covers a connection lost partway through.
.El
.Pp
.Fl h ,
.Fl v ,
.Fl K
and
.Fl W
all exit 0.
.Pp
A script that only cares whether the command worked can check the exit
status alone, without parsing the response.
.Pp
The last case deserves its own note.
Unlike the ordinary request and response flow, a watch that has already
printed real events can still end in failure, if the connection drops
before
.Fl n
is reached, or at any point at all when no
.Fl n
was given, since nothing else ever ends it.
A script telling
.Dq watched to completion
apart from
.Dq the connection dropped partway
checks the exit status, not merely whether any event lines appeared.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Ask which protocol version the server speaks:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ icowm-msg get_version
{"ok":true,"protocol_version":1}
.Ed
.Pp
Switch to another desktop, and the same with one that does not exist:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ icowm-msg goto_desktop desktop_id=1
{"ok":true}

$ icowm-msg goto_desktop desktop_id=99
{"ok":false,"error":"no such desktop on that surface"}
.Ed
.Pp
Move a client, giving its identifier either way round:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ icowm-msg move_client client_id=23068673 x=100 y=200
$ icowm-msg move_client client_id=0x1600001 x=100 y=200
.Ed
.Pp
Act on the exit status alone, ignoring the response:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
if icowm-msg iconify_all desktop_id=0 >/dev/null; then
    echo "iconified everything on desktop 0"
fi
.Ed
.Pp
Pull one field out of a response with any JSON tool:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ icowm-msg get_focused | jq -r '.focused[0].client_id'
23068673
.Ed
.Pp
Watch until the connection drops, which is what the trailing status
reports:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ icowm-msg -w window_mapped
{"ok":true}
{"client_id":23068673,"desktop_id":0,"surface_id":0,"event":"window_mapped"}
icowm-msg: connection closed with no response
$ echo $?
2
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy failed to connect to Ar path
Nothing is listening at the resolved path.
Either
.Xr icowm 1
is not running, or it was started with
.Fl s ,
which disables the socket for that run on purpose, or its socket failed
to come up, which it logs a warning about and continues without, or
.Nm
resolved a different path than the running server bound, most often
because
.Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
differs between the two shells.
.It Sy argument Ar foo Sy is not in key=value form
An argument after the command name had no
.Ql =
in it.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr icowm 1
.Pp
.Pa icowm-msg.md ,
installed under the documentation directory, carries the same command
list with the response each one returns, which this page leaves out.
